{"name":"Ultramarine Green","hex":"#007a64","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"ultramarine","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Beyond the sea.","Of a brilliant dark blue or slightly purplish color like that of the pigment (noun sense 1)."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["In full ultramarine blue: a brilliant blue pigment traditionally made from ground-up lapis lazuli, and now usually either extracted from mineral deposits or made synthetically.","A brilliant dark blue or slightly purplish colour like that of the pigment."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/ultramarine"},{"term":"green","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The color of grass and leaves; a primary additive color midway between yellow and blue which is evoked by light between roughly 495–570 nm.","A member of a green party; an environmentalist."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of a green hue.","Sickly, unwell."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/green"}],"entries":[{"title":"Ultramarine","blocks":[{"text":"Ultramarine is a deep blue pigment historically derived from lazurite, the principal component of the metamorphic rock lapis lazuli. The pigment was produced by grinding the stone and undergoing a complex washing process to isolate the blue mineral, which made it exceptionally valuable."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/67/Ultramarinepigment.jpg/330px-Ultramarinepigment.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ultramarine","wikidata":"Q219660"},{"title":"Green","blocks":[{"text":"Green is the color between cyan and yellow on the visible spectrum. It has 295–350 different shades. It is evoked by light which has a dominant wavelength of roughly 495–570 nm. In subtractive color systems, used in painting and color printing, it is created by a combination of yellow and cyan; in the RGB color model, used on television and computer screens, it is one of the additive primary colors, along with red and blue, which are mixed in different combinations to create all other colors. By far the largest contributor to green in nature is chlorophyll, the chemical by which plants photosynthesize and convert sunlight into chemical energy. Many creatures have adapted to their green environments by taking on a green hue themselves as camouflage. Several minerals have a green color, including the emerald, which is colored green by its chromium content."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/23/Champ_de_bl%C3%A9_C%C3%B4te-d%27Or_Bourgogne_avril_2014.jpg/330px-Champ_de_bl%C3%A9_C%C3%B4te-d%27Or_Bourgogne_avril_2014.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Green","wikidata":"Q3133"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/1/16/Natural_ultramarine_painted.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled 1x","src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/1/16/Natural_ultramarine_painted.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","alt":"Natural ultramarine painted","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ANatural_ultramarine_painted.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/da/Lapis-lazuli_hg.jpg/330px-Lapis-lazuli_hg.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/da/Lapis-lazuli_hg.jpg/960px-Lapis-lazuli_hg.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/da/Lapis-lazuli_hg.jpg/330px-Lapis-lazuli_hg.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ALapis-lazuli_hg.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commo